<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Denbigh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Denbigh is a market town in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vale-of-clwyd">Vale of Clwyd</a>, in the medieval cantref of Rhufoniog. A Welsh stronghold there was held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dafydd-ap-gruffydd">Dafydd ap Gruffudd</a> before the English conquest, after which Henry de Lacy, Earl of Lincoln, built Denbigh Castle and a walled town from 1282. The town later gave its name to the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denbighshire">Denbighshire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/denbigh.jpg" alt="Denbigh" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Buck · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denbigh_Library_(geograph_4696820).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Denbigh, in Welsh Dinbych, meaning Little Fortress, is a market town and community in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denbighshire">Denbighshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, near the Clwydian Hills. It developed around its castle and the walled settlement that followed. Anciently it formed part of the cantref of Rhufoniog, which for much of its history was subordinate to the Kingdom of Gwynedd, though it spent periods under English control in the 12th and 13th centuries. By the 13th century Denbigh was its main town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Following Edward I's conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, Rhufoniog became part of a new marcher lordship called Denbigh or Denbighland, granted in 1284 to Henry de Lacy, Earl of Lincoln. Construction of the castle and town walls began in 1282. The town was caught up in the revolt of Madog ap Llywelyn in 1294-1295: the castle fell in the autumn, a relieving force was defeated on 11 November 1294, and Edward recaptured the town in December. It was burnt in 1400 during the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a>, and the walled town was largely destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a>, after which settlement moved to a market place outside the walls along High Street.</p><p>The Laws in Wales Act 1535 created the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denbighshire">Denbighshire</a> in 1536 with Denbigh as county town, its courts held alternately there and at Wrexham. The County Hall, now the library, was built in 1572. In 1579 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-dudley-1st-earl-of-leicester">Robert Dudley, 1st Earl of Leicester</a> and Baron of Denbigh, planned a cathedral to take city status from nearby St Asaph, but the money ran out and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>'s Church remains unfinished. A Royalist garrison held the town in 1643, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a>, and surrendered in 1646, after which the castle and walls fell into ruin. In the 1600s the town grew around textiles, with glovers, weavers, saddlers and tanners.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The medieval walls, castle ruins and historic centre still shape the town, which had a population of about 8,669 at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Its parish church of St Marcella stands a mile east of the walled town. The North Wales Hospital, founded in the 1840s for psychiatric patients, was the town's largest employer until it closed in 1995. Denbigh hosted the National Eisteddfod of Wales in 1882, 1939, 2001 and 2013.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Denbigh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was one of several small early kingdoms of north Wales whose local dynasties appear in medieval genealogies, and its territory included the hills around <b>Denbigh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-rhufoniog">Kingdom of Rhufoniog</a></li></ul></section>
